#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:2
msgid "How to link to other documentation projects with Intersphinx"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:4
msgid ""
"This section shows you how to maintain references to named sections of "
"other external Sphinx projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:6
msgid ""
"You may be familiar with using the :ref:`:ref: role <sphinx:ref-role>` to"
" :doc:`link to any location of your docs </guides/cross-referencing-with-"
"sphinx>`. It helps you to keep all links within your docs up to date and "
"warns you if a reference target moves or changes so you can ensure that "
"your docs don't have broken cross-references."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:11
msgid ""
"Sometimes you may need to link to a specific section of another project's"
" documentation. While you could just hyperlink directly, there is a "
"better way. :doc:`Intersphinx <sphinx:usage/extensions/intersphinx>` "
"allows you to use all :ref:`cross-reference roles <sphinx:xref-syntax>` "
"from Sphinx with objects in other projects. That is, you could use the "
"``:ref:`` role to link to sections of other documentation projects. "
"Sphinx will ensure that your cross-references to the other project exist "
"and will raise a warning if they are deleted or changed so you can keep "
"your docs up to date."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:17
msgid ""
"If you are publishing several Sphinx projects together using Read the "
"Docs' *subprojects* (see :doc:`/subprojects`), you should use Intersphinx"
" to reference your subprojects from other projects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:22
msgid ""
"You can also use Sphinx's ``linkcheck`` builder to check for broken "
"links. By default it will also check the validity of ``#anchors`` in "
"links."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:29
msgid ""
"See all the `options for the linkcheck builder <https://www.sphinx-"
"doc.org/en/master/usage/configuration.html#options-for-the-linkcheck-"
"builder>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:32
msgid "Using Intersphinx"
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:34
msgid ""
"To use Intersphinx you need to add it to the list of extensions in your "
"``conf.py`` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:44
msgid ""
"And use the ``intersphinx_mapping`` configuration to indicate the name "
"and link of the projects you want to use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../upstream/docs/user/guides/intersphinx.rst:64
msgid ""
"If you are using Read the Docs' subprojects, you also need to enable the "
"Intersphinx extension on each of the subprojects. For each subproject, "
"you need to add the main project and all the other subprojects to "
"``intersphinx_mapping``."
msgstr ""

